An archetype, not a replica
---------------------------

This cartridge models a FAMILY — a medium that plugs in by a contact edge: a
shell split on its mid-plane around a board, a contact window, label recesses,
keys, a door and a pass-through slot, or a monolithic thin card — which is ours
to license. It does not model any manufacturer's cartridge or card.

Named formats appear only as PRESETS generated from the keystone's
reference-objects catalog (hyperobjects-spec `hyperobjects_reference`). Every
number in such a preset is either a DIMENSION OR COUNT THE SOURCE PUBLISHED, with
its tier, URL, date and quote recorded in the catalog; a value DERIVED from one by
a written archetype rule that is ours (docs/README.md); or an ARCHETYPE DEFAULT
that is ours outright. Nothing else.

When this cartridge was first published the pinned catalog filed NO object in
this family. It therefore ships five ARCHETYPE presets (`kind: archetype`):
top-loading cartridge, front-loading cartridge, card, pass-through cartridge and
shuttered cartridge. They are named points in the family's own parameter space.
Their dimensions are round numbers chosen by us; they assert nothing about any
product, and no product should be read into them.

This cartridge reproduces no logo, no wordmark, no label artwork, no colourway,
no texture and no maker's grip or rib pattern. A label is a flat RECESS. Grip
ridges are a generic evenly pitched groove set. Keying notches are rectangles
whose count and size come from the archetype rules unless a source publishes
them. **A preset is a fit envelope, not a likeness.** It exists so a console
slot, a case pocket, a storage rack or a replacement shell can be built to the
right size.


What is ours, specifically
--------------------------

  * Corner radius, wall thickness, board thickness and clearances.
  * The contact window's depth, and its width wherever no contact count and
    pitch are published.
  * Label recess sizes and positions; grip ridges in their entirety.
  * Keying notch count, size and position (the catalog contract has no notch
    field yet).
  * The shutter door's outline and the pass-through slot's size: the catalog
    records only whether a format has them.
  * The grip band: the contract publishes one shell depth, so a sourced preset
    carries a uniform thickness.


